.TH PCFG 1 "" "pcfg" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pcfg \- show and edit Promptuarium configuration files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pcfg new
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-comment
.IR TEXT ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B pcfg show
.I FILE
.RI [ KEY ]
.RB [ \-\-flat ]
.RB [ \-\-max\-bytes
.IR N ]
.br
.B pcfg get
.I FILE KEY
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-out
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B pcfg set
.I FILE KEY VALUE
.RB [ \-\-comment
.IR TEXT ]
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B pcfg delete
.I FILE KEY
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B pcfg rename
.I FILE KEY NEW\-NAME
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B pcfg comment
.I FILE KEY
.RI [ TEXT ]
.RB [ \-\-add ]
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B pcfg import
.I FILE JSON\-FILE\fR|\fB\-
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.br
.B pcfg export
.I FILE
.RI [ KEY ]
.RB [ \-\-output
.IR JSON\-FILE ]
.br
.B pcfg check
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-strict ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pcfg
shows and edits configuration files of Promptuarium: containers signed
.B PRMT:CFG1\-LE
in the layout of the serializer (SERIALIZER_FORMAT.md). It knows the tags (NAME, TYPE, ITEM, VALUE, COMMENT) and the
type codes (NULL, BOOLEAN, OBJECT, ARRAY, FLOAT64, UTF8_STRING, BLOB), so a configuration is shown by its values, not
by its nodes:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# Settings of the app
Logging {
  LogLevel {
    # the default level
    Default = "Information"
  }
}
Hosts [
  0 = "a.example"
]
Port = 8080
Key = hex:DEADBEEF
.RE
.fi
.PP
Every command is a single, non\-interactive call. A file is checked before it is shown or edited; an invalid file is
refused, and
.B check
lists every problem of it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B new \fIFILE\fR
Creates a configuration without members.
.TP
.B show \fIFILE\fR [\fIKEY\fR]
Shows the configuration, or the value of the key, as a tree; the comments are the
.B #
lines before the value they are about. With
.BR \-\-flat ,
every value is a
.I key = value
line with its full key. A string is quoted, a BLOB is shown as
.BI hex: ...
(shortened to
.B \-\-max\-bytes
bytes, 32 by default).
.TP
.B get \fIFILE KEY\fR
Prints a value as it is: a string without quotes, a BLOB as hex, a number,
.BR true ,
.B false
or
.BR null ;
an object or an array as a tree.
.B \-\-json
prints the value as JSON,
.B \-\-out
writes the bytes of the value into a file.
.TP
.B set \fIFILE KEY VALUE\fR
Sets a value. The missing objects on the way are created; an index of an array must exist or be the next one. The
comments of an existing value are kept;
.B \-\-comment
replaces them.
.TP
.B delete \fIFILE KEY\fR
Deletes a value (every member with the name, so that an earlier one does not come back).
.TP
.B rename \fIFILE KEY NEW\-NAME\fR
Renames a member of an object.
.TP
.B comment \fIFILE KEY\fR [\fITEXT\fR]
Replaces the comments of a value (of the file with the key
.BR '' )
with the text.
.B \-\-add
appends the text; without a text the comments are removed.
.TP
.B import \fIFILE JSON\-FILE\fR
Creates a configuration from a JSON file (or the standard input,
.BR \- ),
e.g. an appsettings.json. Comments and trailing commas are allowed; the root must be an object.
.TP
.B export \fIFILE\fR [\fIKEY\fR]
Writes the configuration, or a value, as JSON. A BLOB is a base64 string and the comments are left out, so the JSON
is not a full copy of the file.
.TP
.B check \fIFILE\fR
Checks the file by SERIALIZER_FORMAT.md: the signature, the tags, the type codes (a reserved one is an error), the
holders, the values and the comments. A repeated name is a warning: the last one wins.
.B \-\-strict
makes a warning an error.
.SH KEYS
A key is the names of the members and the indexes of the elements, separated by
.B :
as in IConfiguration, e.g.
.B Logging:LogLevel:Default
or
.BR Hosts:0 .
A
.B :
or a
.B \e
in a name is escaped with a
.BR \e .
The empty key
.B ''
is the root.
.SH VALUES
.TP
.BI string: TEXT
A string.
.TP
.BI number: 1.5
A number (FLOAT64).
.TP
.BR bool:true ", " bool:false
A boolean.
.TP
.BR null ", " {} ", " []
Null, an empty object, an empty array.
.TP
.BI hex: DEADBEEF\fR,\fP " base64:" 3q2+7w==\fR,\fP " file:" PATH
A BLOB.
.TP
.BI json: JSON
Any JSON value, e.g. an object with its members.
.PP
A value without a form keeps the type of the existing value (so
.B set app.cfg Port 9090
stays a number, and a string stays a string). A new value is a boolean
.RB ( true ", " false ),
a number (as in JSON, e.g. 8080), or else a string.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-signature \fISIG\fR
The signature of the file (every command takes it):
.B PRMT:CFG1\-LE
by default, e.g.
.B PRMT:GEN1\-LE
for general objects.
.TP
.B \-\-output \fIFILE\fR
An editing command saves the file there, not in place. A file is always written into a temporary file first.
.TP
.B \-\-
The arguments after it are not options, e.g. a value that starts with
.BR \-\- .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid command line: unknown command or option, missing argument, invalid key or value.
.TP
.B 2
The file or the key does not exist.
.TP
.B 3
The operation failed: invalid file (errors with
.BR check ,
or warnings with
.BR "check \-\-strict" ),
existing file, a key through a scalar, I/O error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pedit (1),
.BR ppak (1)
